</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.5.1 Dissecting the Reward Function ‣ 5.5 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Visual Programmability: A Guide for Code-as-Thought in Chart Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.5.1 Dissecting the Reward Function ‣ 5.5 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Visual Programmability: A Guide for Code-as-Thought in Chart Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> demonstrate the synergy between our reward components. The <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision Reward (<math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{decision}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">decision</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{decision}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> is essential for preventing mode collapse. Without it, the model defaults to a single strategy—either 0% or 100% code usage—leading to poor performance on certain benchmarks (e.g., a 4.2 point drop on CharXiv).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{decision}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>decision</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{decision}}</annotation></semantics></math> teaches the model <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span> to use a tool, the <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Accuracy Reward (<math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> teaches it <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> to use it well. Without <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model becomes overly cautious on programmable charts (e.g., only 50.4% code usage on ChartX). The full reward function encourages a balanced and confident policy, leading to the best overall performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Code usage percentage in the reward ablation study. The decision reward (<math id="S5.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{decision}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>decision</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{decision}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is critical for preventing mode collapse and enabling adaptive behavior. All values are percentages (%).</figcaption>
